# NDJSON (newline delimited JSON)

NDJSON is a convenient format for storing or streaming
structured data that may be processed one record at a time. It works well with unix-style
text processing tools and shell pipelines. It’s a great format for log files.
It’s also a flexible format for passing messages between cooperating processes.

1. Line Separator is '/n'

This means '/r/n' is also supported because trailing white space is ignored when parsing JSON values.
 
2. Each Line is a Valid JSON Value

The most common values will be objects or arrays, but any JSON value is permitted. See json.org for more information about JSON values.

Any internal line feed characters must be escaped. OR better yet, just don't use them.

NDJSON authoring: line feeds and practical workflow

Rule: NDJSON = one JSON object per line, terminated by a newline (\n).

No pretty‑printing across multiple lines.

Newlines inside strings must be escaped (\\n), not literal line breaks.

Do you hand‑edit NDJSON? Rarely.

Typically generated by code (client/server).

For manual probes (netcat/telnet), you paste a single compact JSON and hit Enter once.

Editor gotchas:

Turn off auto‑wrapping/formatting for NDJSON files.

Ensure each object is on exactly one line.

Trailing newline at end of the line is fine (and expected).

If you must store fixtures, prefer a .ndjson file with multiple lines, each a complete object.

Examples

Valid NDJSON (two messages, two lines):

{"id":"1","name":"system.handshake.request","meta":{"protocol":"0.1.0"},"payload":{"client_version":"0.1.0"}}
{"id":"1","name":"system.welcome","meta":{"protocol":"0.1.0"},"payload":{"server_version":"0.1.0","session_id":"...","motd":"Welcome"}}

Invalid (pretty‑printed across lines → breaks framing):

{
  "id": "1",
  "name": "system.handshake.request",
  ...
}

